-- title:
--   The full modular function field is a curve over K
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a perfect field and let $N$ be a nonzero natural number. Inside the Laurent series field $K((q))$ consider the intermediate field $F = \mathtt{modularFunctionFieldFullC}\;K\;N$ obtained by adjoining to $K$ the set of those Laurent series of the form $\mathtt{qExpand}\,K\,d\,(\mathtt{jqModC}\,K)$, i.e. the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant with coefficients in $K$ with $q$ replaced by $q^{d}$, for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$. The assertion is that the extension $F/K$ satisfies the predicate [`AlgebraicCurve.IsCurveOver`](def/AlgebraicCurve_IsCurveOver.html#L15), that is: (i) principal divisors exist and have degree zero — for every $f \in F$ with $f \neq 0$ there is a divisor $D$ of $F/K$ whose value at each place $v$ (a valuation subring of $F$ containing $K$, distinct from $F$ itself, and a principal ideal ring) equals $v.\mathrm{ord}\,f$, and with $\deg D = 0$; (ii) for every place $v$ the residue field of $v$ is a finite-dimensional $K$-module; and (iii) the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega_{F/K}$ is free of rank $1$ over $F$.
--
--   This records that the field generated over $K$ by the $q$-expansions $j(q^{d})$, $d \mid N$, is an algebraic function field in one variable over $K$ in the axiomatic sense used throughout the project, so that the divisor and differential machinery applies to the modular curve $X_0(N)$ over an arbitrary perfect base field. It underlies the work with places, reductions and charts on models of modular curves in characteristic $p$, and is invoked widely in that part of the development; the proof cites the criterion for a function field generated by a transcendental element with finite separable top extension, together with the existence of modular polynomial data for $N$.
